{"kind":"report","text":"Approved exact head dec4b2feb870de5a49438621fefd446f619e8290 for #19212, reviewed under #19291. The selected implementation Report is #19289 (d318ab88); companion #19247 (ffcda49b) accounts for the other changed path.\n\nThe correction in [sandbox_test.go](https://github.com/generalbusiness-ai/gitseq/blob/dec4b2feb870de5a49438621fefd446f619e8290/spike/querysandbox/sandbox_test.go#L117) requires both the cancellation sentinel and a still-live caller. The same predicate rejects a real SQLite query cancelled by an already-expired caller with the internal guard disabled. The existing guarded, disabled-guard and restored-guard queries remain bounded. The [README](https://github.com/generalbusiness-ai/gitseq/blob/dec4b2feb870de5a49438621fefd446f619e8290/spike/querysandbox/README.md#L49) explains why the shared sentinel alone is insufficient.\n\nIndependent validation: in a clean detached checkout, go test -race -count=1 ./spike/querysandbox passed (2.348s); go vet ./spike/querysandbox passed. A Go overlay removing only callerErr != nil from the predicate failed at the named negative control: “caller watchdog satisfied the internal-deadline assertion” (0.330s). My first overlay used /tmp instead of Go's resolved /private/tmp path and therefore did not apply; that run is not mutation evidence. Correcting the overlay path produced the expected failure without changing the candidate. CI34028135444 and Security34028135527 independently report success for this exact head; the attached CI record shows all 35 packages passing fresh race tests and the required build, vet, UI, reproducibility and clone-build gates. I read the retained inherited failures at cc24 (5.04s) and publication133 (5.02s); the outer watchdog was accepted by the former predicate. These failures remain evidence, not replaced by isolated passes.\n\nArchitecture: this isolated spike test and its documentation preserve all seven production contracts in docs/reference/architecture.md: ordinary Git storage; kernel; Nexus and live runtime; application host binding; application profile and interpreter; projections and queries; CLI, MCP, skills, connectors and UI. The spike is not wired into production. No architecture contract changes, so no architecture-page amendment is required. Compared with then-current main1b0692eef275d51283069f0f4dbf47346ee442c2, the intervening publication change is disjoint: sandbox source, dependencies and both changed paths have not moved from the candidate's base.\n\nSecurity: the control closes false assurance at the caller/internal deadline boundary using the actual query binding. Query restores its previous interrupt context, so the expired-caller control does not poison the subsequent mutant query. The production five-millisecond deadline, read-only connection, authorizer, row/byte bounds, signatures, custody and authority remain unchanged. No unresolved security defect found. This is assigned test correction, not follow-on authority obtained through a decision-commission chain.\n\nSimplification: one small shared predicate and one deterministic expired-caller control are sufficient. No extra timeout, retry, scheduler sleep, production hook or duplicate proof framework is introduced; no further simplification is needed to meet the conditions.\n\nBoth exact-path artifacts are fresh, effective and unretired at review. Requester ratification, sealed merge with exact-path succession, push and owned-checkout cleanup remain required. This verdict authorizes no resident activation.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:d318ab88a5d0aa1973db5b961fae3765c4d42343","head":"dec4b2feb870de5a49438621fefd446f619e8290","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:2cfc62248f2c8c8d7e51653692e531e2cf747cb4\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:196eeae9efb6ef896be2f7d7bc599b932e7614d1","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"approved"}}